UPDATE companies SET description='Nasoya, now operating as Pulmuone Nasoya Foods USA, traces its roots to 1978 when its founders began producing tofu in a converted barn in Leominster, Massachusetts. In 2004, the brand was acquired by South Korea’s Pulmuone Foods, a global leader in plant-based foods, and the combined entity has since expanded its U.S. footprint. Today the company is headquartered in Ayer, Massachusetts, where a new facility slated to open in 2025 is expected to become the world’s largest tofu production line.

The company manufactures a range of plant-based products, including multiple tofu varieties (silken, firm, extra-firm, super-firm), Asian-inspired items such as noodles, dumplings, and wonton wraps, and ready-to-eat meals under the Plantspired label. Its operations emphasize organic, non-GMO soybeans sourced from local growers, and the facility incorporates sustainable practices such as tray-free packaging, brine recycling, and wastewater treatment that processes 34.6 million gallons annually. Historical data notes a 1987 facility capable of producing more than 60,000 pounds of tofu per week.

The brand also engages foodservice and culinary channels through partnerships with institutions like the Culinary Institute of America.

The company states that its differentiation lies in a commitment to organic sourcing, environmental stewardship, and making plant-based eating accessible. Its mission centers on delivering wholesome, convenient foods that fit diverse lifestyles while reducing resource use—citing tofu’s 85% lower water footprint compared to beef.', certifications=ARRAY[]::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Tofu production (silken, firm, extra-firm, super-firm)", "Plant-based meal manufacturing (Plantspired line)", "Noodle production", "Dumpling and wonton wrap production", "Ready-to-eat meal assembly", "Sustainable packaging (tray-free)", "Brine recycling", "Wastewater treatment", "Soy byproduct reuse", "Organic ingredient sourcing"], "industries": ["Food and beverage manufacturing", "Plant-based protein", "Retail grocery", "Natural and organic foods"], "materials": ["Organic soybeans", "Non-GMO ingredients"], "equipment": [], "volume": "60,000+ pounds of tofu per week (1987 facility)", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["Organic, non-GMO soybeans sourced from local farmers", "Recycles 80 tons of cardboard annually", "Treats 34.6 million gallons of wastewater each year", "Reuses 3.4 million pounds of soy byproduct annually", "Introducing tray-free tofu packaging (2024)", "Planning world\u2019s largest tofu production line in Ayer, MA (2025)"], "facts_used": ["Founded 1978 in Leominster, MA -> /about-us", "Acquired by Pulmuone Foods in 2004 -> /about-us", "Headquarters in Ayer, MA -> db", "Produces >60,000 lbs tofu/week (1987) -> /about-us", "Products in 25,000+ stores nationwide -> /", "Organic, non-GMO soybeans from local farmers -> /about-us", "Sustainability: recycles 80 tons cardboard, treats 34.6M gallons wastewater, reuses 3.4M lbs soy annually -> /about-us", "Tray-free packaging introduced 2024 -> /about-us", "World''s largest tofu production line planned 2025 in Ayer, MA -> /about-us", "LinkedIn URL -> db"], "company_type": "Manufacturer", "makes_products": "yes", "type_evidence": "Nasoya began in 1978 when its founders started making tofu in a converted barn in Leominster, MA.", "founded_year": "1978", "employee_count": "", "cage_code": "", "min_order": "", "export_compliance": [], "languages": [], "social_links": ["https://www.linkedin.com/company/nasoya-foods-usa/"], "ownership_tags": []}'::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug='pulmuone-nasoya-foods-usa' AND approved AND profile IS NULL;
UPDATE companies SET description='Purposeful Design operates a wood and metal manufacturing facility in Indianapolis, Indiana. Established as a Christian ministry, the organization provides employment and job training to men transitioning from homelessness, addiction, and incarceration. The ministry was founded to rebuild lives through the dignity of work, though a specific founding year is not publicly disclosed.

The company produces solid wood furniture including tables, desks, benches, and other commercial pieces, as well as wood tops such as tabletops, countertops, shelving, and stair treads. It also supplies OEM component parts and offers surface refinishing services. All production occurs in a combined wood and metal shop that supports both custom commercial projects and residential orders.

Customers include businesses seeking commercial furniture for offices and hospitality, residential buyers, and OEMs needing component parts. The organization’s “Proven Process” and “Product and Design Guide” support collaborative projects from concept through delivery. Its job-training program, called Advance!, integrates workforce development with production.

Purposeful Design distinguishes itself by using a job-creation and revenue-generation model: sales of the furniture made by program participants fund the wrap-around support services—discipleship, community, mentorship, housing, and transportation—that help employees achieve self-sufficiency. This marketplace approach is described as bringing “marketplace rigor and leverage” to life change.', certifications=ARRAY[]::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Solid wood furniture manufacturing", "Wood top fabrication (tabletops, countertops, shelving, stair treads)", "Surface refinishing", "OEM component part production", "Metal fabrication", "Job training and workforce development (Advance! program)", "Product design and engineering support", "Custom commercial furniture production", "Residential furniture manufacturing"], "industries": ["Commercial furniture", "Residential furniture", "OEM manufacturing", "Wood products"], "materials": ["Solid wood", "Metal"], "equipment": [], "volume": "", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["Job-creation and revenue-generation model funding life-rebuilding programs", "Wood and metal manufacturing facility employing men from homeless missions", "Wrap-around support including discipleship, community, mentorship, housing, transportation", "Commercial furniture, wood tops, OEM component parts, and residential goods under one roof", "Proven process for quality manufacturing and collaborative product design"], "facts_used": ["Headquarters: Indianapolis, Indiana -> db", "Christian ministry model -> /about", "Wood and metal manufacturing facility -> /about", "Products: commercial furniture, wood tops, OEM component parts, residential goods -> /", "Job training program (Advance!) -> /about", "Wrap-around support services -> /about", "Marketplace rigor and revenue generation model -> /about", "Solid wood furniture manufacturer -> /", "OEM supply capability -> /", "Refinishing surfaces -> /", "Proven process and Product and Design Guide -> /", "Materials: wood and metal -> /about", "Industries served: commercial furniture, residential, OEM -> /", "LinkedIn URL -> db"], "company_type": "Manufacturer", "makes_products": "yes", "type_evidence": "We welcome men coming from the streets through homeless missions who want a better life, offering them a job in our wood + metal manufacturing facility.", "founded_year": "", "employee_count": "", "cage_code": "", "min_order": "", "export_compliance": [], "languages": [], "social_links": ["https://www.linkedin.com/company/purposeful-design/"], "ownership_tags": []}'::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug='purposeful-design' AND approved AND profile IS NULL;
UPDATE companies SET description='Pyramid Packaging, Inc. is based in Northbrook, Illinois. The company was incorporated in December 1968 by James S. Ploen, a U.S. Army veteran who envisioned a family-owned business that could supply packaging machinery, materials, and service expertise from a single source. Ploen’s background included door-to-door encyclopedia sales in Alaska, and he launched the venture with key supplier partnerships from Automated Packaging Systems (Autobag) and American Packaging Machinery (AMPAK).

Today the company provides flexible packaging materials such as gusseted poly bags for bulk and irregular products, and it represents manufacturers of packaging equipment. Its service portfolio includes packaging line layout engineering, factory-trained technician repairs and preventative maintenance, equipment training, rental and lease programs, on-site demonstrations, flexible packaging inventory management, and custom packaging design with artwork development. A brand‑new showroom hosts equipment demonstrations.

Pyramid Packaging serves manufacturers and e‑commerce fulfillment operations that need to package products for shipment. The company’s own materials note that virtually every producer packages goods for sale, and that the growth of e‑commerce has increased demand for parcel‑level shipping solutions.

